Using automem:
--------------

1) Update or create the ram definitions in the ram-config file.

Edit the ram-config file to describe the ram devices to be used. In
this example we shall add a data ram for the instruction cache. The
ram to add is called Ram32byte_512. The header of its Verilog
model is as follows:

module Ram32byte_512 (Q, CLK, CEN, WEN, A, D, OEN);
  output [31: 0] Q;   // 32 bit wide data.
  input          CLK; // Clock.
  input          CEN; // Chip select.
  input  [ 3: 0] WEN; // Byte word-enables.
  input  [ 8: 0] A;   // 512 address locations.
  input  [31: 0] D;   // 32 bit wide data.
  input          OEN; // Output enable, active low.

The details of this ram must be defined in the ram-config file. Each
definition starts with the keyword "ram_definition" followed by the
ram name, a list of pin keyword and pin name pairs and finally the
keyword "end_ram_definition". The definition for the above ram is as
follows:

ram_definition Ram32byte_512
   clock = CLK
   chip_select = CEN
   address = A[8:0]
   read_data = Q[31:0]
   write_data = D[31:0]
   write_enable = WEN[3:0]
   not_output_enable = OEN
end_ram_definition

The width of each input or output is defined by the Verilog-style
range after the pin name, no range implies that the pin is only a
single bit. If a pin is active-low the pin keyword should be prefixed
with "not_" as for the output enable pin above. The output_enable pin
(OEN in this case) is the only one in the above example that is
optional.

2) Update the ram-config file to associate rams with their functions.

Following up the example above for the ram Ram32byte_512, we need to
associate this ram with its desired function. In this case it will be
the data ram for the instruction cache. This is set using the "icdata"
keyword followed by the ram name:

icdata     Ram32byte_512

The ram keywords and their meanings are:

icdata        Instruction cache data ram.
ictag         Instruction cache tag ram.
icvalid       Instruction cache valid ram.
dcdata        Data cache data ram.
dctag         Data cache tag ram.
dcvalid       Data cache valid ram.
dcdirty       Data cache dirty ram.
mmu112bit     MMU (112 bit ram option)
mmu128bit     MMU (128 bit ram option)
mmu30bit      MMU (30 and 26 bit ram option)
mmu26bit      MMU (30 and 26 bit ram option)

The MMU configuration is more complex than the others and is described
below.

3) Update the ram-config file to set various options:

The option keywords, their meanings and possible values are as follows:

replacement       Sets the replacement algorithm used by both caches.
                  This can be either "random" or "round-robin". For the
                  former the width of the valid ram needs to be 16 bits
                  and for the latter it needs to be 24 bits.
                  
clockgating       This sets the block-level clock gating. Can be either
                  "true" or "false".
                  
optimise          When instantiating multiple banks of ram to create a
                  deeper memory, the extra circuitry to control
                  the rams can be optimised either for low-power or
                  high-speed. Use "power" and "speed" to switch
                  optimisations.
                  
dcsize            These two options set the size of the data cache and
icsize            the instruction cache respectively. Set to a value such
                  as 8k or 32k etc.

4) Run the automem script.

The automem script takes three parameters. Firstly the source Verilog to
which the rams will be added (and any existing rams removed). Secondly,
an output Verilog file where the output will be placed. Finally, the
ram-config file that you created in steps 1-3. You should run automem
from the MAIN_ARM926EJS directory.

./automem ../ARM926EJS/ARM926EJS.v ARM926EJS_8k_ICache_16k_DCache.v ram-config

In this example the output will be placed into
ARM926EJS_8k_ICache_16k_DCache.v and two other files will also be
created: ARM926EJS_8k_ICache_16k_DCache.vh and
ARM926EJS_8k_ICache_16k_DCache.vc. The latter of these can be edited
to point to the location of Verilog files used to simulate the rams.

5) Link the new files.

So that the new files can be found they should be linked to
ARM926EJS.v, ARM926EJS.vh and ARM926EJS.vc in the MAIN_ARM926EJS
directory, for example:

ln -s ARM926EJS_8k_ICache_16k_DCache.v  ARM926EJS.v
ln -s ARM926EJS_8k_ICache_16k_DCache.vh ARM926EJS.vh
ln -s ARM926EJS_8k_ICache_16k_DCache.vc ARM926EJS.vc

Capabilities of automem:
------------------------

The automem script is capable of instantiating multiple banks of ram
when either the ram given is too narrow or not deep enough. For
instance when a 16k data cache is required but only a ram 512 deep
(like the Ram32byte_512 example above) is avaliable. Any supporting
circuitry required for this will be automatically generated. The
script can also cope with some cases of the write-enable widths of the
rams not matching those of the core interface. When ram signals are
active-low they will automatically be inverted so long as the "not_"
prefix was used in the ram definition.

MMU rams:
---------

There are three options for MMU rams:

(1) define a 112 bit wide ram with bit write enables, or
(2) define a 128 bit wide ram with word write enables, or
(3) define 30 bit and 26 bit rams.

For option (1) define mmu112bit, for option (2) define mmu128bit and
for option (3) define mmu30bit and mmu26bit. The others should either
be omitted or set to "none".

Adding rams to a modified design:
---------------------------------

If the ARM926EJS top-level is modified, for example to add BIST, the
signal names to which the rams should be connected will change. In
order that automem can use these new signal names the memory-config
file should be edited. This file does not need to be modified if no
extra circuitry is added or if the extra circuitry is added after
automem has been used to add the rams to the ARM926EJS top-level.
